ach (cmd-33da49dc138147cf, cmd-0cb0483d63c249cd, cmd-a6e0c1ea2cb8463f), and recovered service/data/replication state was directly confirmed on each node afterward (cmd-f060e25430a942c6, cmd-abf9385759bc42ea, cmd-04a580e6e96943d0, cmd-62ac17b61ffd49d1, cmd-711d72c872f740a4, cmd-ff79317f59b04571), including a post-restart primary write propagating to both standbys. The fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot boot identities match the executor-observed post-restart boot_ids for all three nodes, corroborating the transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a90da5cd587e44b6","cmd-8b9bf29d9a244aa5","cmd-9d7f6192306b4bb5","cmd-0bd8095953914c8f","cmd-2d15bb10b46b46a5","cmd-786bc413b79e402c","cmd-33da49dc138147cf","cmd-0cb0483d63c249cd","cmd-a6e0c1ea2cb8463f","cmd-f060e25430a942c6","cmd-abf9385759bc42ea","cmd-04a580e6e96943d0","cmd-62ac17b61ffd49d1","cmd-711d72c872f740a4","cmd-ff79317f59b04571","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.6,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were purposeful (installs, config edits, cert issuance/deployment, basebackup, restarts) and directly tied to the task. Two notable issues: (1) The cluster CA private key (ca.key) and per-node/replicator private keys generated in /tmp/pgca on node1 (cmd-7694f4e97087465d, cmd-e7f7e2deb8cc4cd0, cmd-cbe01f2ee20242f6) were left in place with group/other-readable permissions (observed as -rw-rw-r-- in cmd-7694f4e97087465d's directory listing) and never cleaned up or permission-restricted, and equivalent copies of node/replicator private keys were left under /tmp on node2 and node3 (cmd-b9eab7fc31d54d0b, cmd-4fa38f61a6d746c9, cmd-c289c16ca9d94df7) — this is unremoved, security-relevant residue (a locally-readable copy of cluster-signing key material) rather than a bounded reverted action. (2) The rogue/self-signed test certificate used to validate CA enforcement was properly cleaned up afterward (cmd-11a4f648a5024503, rm -f /tmp/rogue.key /tmp/rogue.crt) — a well-bounded, fully reverted diagnostic action, not penalized. Several early failed attempts (permission errors copying certs, sed syntax errors, wal_level typo, recovery.conf home-dir permission errors) were read/no-op failures immediately corrected, not unwanted mutations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7694f4e97087465d","cmd-e7f7e2deb8cc4cd0","cmd-cbe01f2ee20242f6","cmd-b9eab7fc31d54d0b","cmd-4fa38f61a6d746c9","cmd-c289c16ca9d94df7","cmd-11a4f648a5024503"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task instruction are satisfied with direct evidence: TLS-encrypted streaming replication from node1 (writable primary) to node2/node3, backed by a purpose-created cluster CA, with pg_hba.conf restricted to CA-issued client certificates and this restriction actively demonstrated by rejecting a self-signed certificate. End-to-end data replication was verified, and the independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with coherent pre/post boot-identity, acknowledgement, and recovery evidence corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The main deduction is operational hygiene: cluster CA and node/replicator private key material was left behind in /tmp on all three nodes with weak (group/other-readable) permissions and never cleaned up, which is a security-relevant residue independent of task functionality.","limitations":["Verification relies entirely on executor-reported command transcripts (psql output, openssl/pg_hba output); no independent tool access was available to re-verify certificate chains, file permissions system-wide, or current pg_hba.conf/postgresql.conf contents.","The exact live file permissions of the leftover /tmp key material on node2/node3 were not explicitly re-listed after copying (only inferred from analogous listings on node1 and standard scp/cp behavior), so the precise exposure severity of that residue is not fully confirmed."]},"ttft